<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Behistun Inscription</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Behistun Inscription is a monumental rock relief and text carved on a cliff in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> on the orders of the Achaemenid king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darius-i">Darius I</a>, recording his accession and victories. It is written in three languages: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-persian">Old Persian</a>, Elamite and Babylonian. Its Elamite version shows how the administrative language of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elamite-confederation">Elamite Confederation</a> was absorbed into the Persian state, and the trilingual text was key to deciphering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/behistun-inscription.jpg" alt="Behistun Inscription" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Korosh.091 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%DA%A9%D8%AA%DB%8C%D8%A8%D9%87_%D8%AF%D8%A7%D8%B1%DB%8C%D9%88%D8%B4_(_%D8%A8%DB%8C%D8%B3%D8%AA%D9%88%D9%86(.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
